A||||5||drednot||dip stick snaped off!!  HELP!||||1||
Z||000000||drednot||06-07-2001||04:44 PM||||While removing my dipstick tube, it snapped right off flush at the block!!  This car is not a fiero, but I figured I would see if anyone here could help me.  I have a new dip stick tube ready to go in, but I just can't get the old broken part out of the block.  I just wanted to know, could I just drill out the old tube, and put my new one in?  I would of change the oil and filter twice after this.  what do you all think?  does this sound like a good plan?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Scott||06-07-2001||05:06 PM||||Try using a sheet metal screw that barely fits into the hole.  Screw it in, leave 'bout 1 cm out, and use a claw hammer or similar tool to pull it out.  If that does work, I'd use a punch to hammer it loose of the hole and a needle nose to pull it out.  Personally, I'd only consider drilling it as a last resort.  Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||06-07-2001||05:40 PM||||I'd also consider drilling as a last resort. Gotta watch out. If those sparks hit the oil...just be careful.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">   <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||11
Z||000003||drednot||06-07-2001||08:40 PM||||Well, the oil is not the problem.  If you take a blow torch to the oil, it won't burn.  I am woried about the metal.||||reg||10
Z||000004||maryjane||06-07-2001||09:42 PM||||You are right to worry about the metal cuttings from drilling the tube out. I don't know what kind of engine you have but it doesn't take much to plug the pickup screen on some, to say nothing of what a metal shaving can do to oil pump gears,main seals and such if it were to get sloshed up into these areas. If it is not too big of a problem to get the pan off, I'd say go ahead. But if you can get the pan off, you can probably push the tube out from the bottom. If you do decide to drill it,get a bit little larger than the id of the tube and once the bit is started, hold back on the drill, and it may just pull the tube out. Are you sure it isn't threaded? If it is, use a reverse fluted bit(left hand) and run the drill in reverse. Another problem with drilling it out-if any of the tube extends into the pan area, that part will probably fall into the pan and roll around with the oil sloshing. Might get under or on moving parts. I'd try other options before drilling.<BR>Don<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 06-07-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000005||GT GT 87||06-07-2001||09:52 PM|||| Go to the hardware store and get a set of "easy out's" . They are for removing broken bolts and screws . They come in different diameters . Get the type that have the reverce thread type , you will also need a tap handle to grip the easy out . This WILL work , I have done the same thing !  ||||reg||1
